<BaseLayout title="BL23 (Shenshu) — Kidney Shu Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat BL23 (Shenshu) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points/" }, { label: "BL23 (Shenshu)", href: "/points/bl23-shenshu-acupressure-point/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">BL23 (Shenshu) — Kidney Shu Point</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">BL23, known as Shenshu or the Kidney Shu point, is one of the most important points on the Bladder meridian. Located on the lower back, it is traditionally used to support kidney function, relieve low back pain, and restore energy.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Bladder (BL)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Lower back, about two finger-widths beside the second lumbar vertebra (L2)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Low back pain and weakness, fatigue, knee weakness, tinnitus, frequent urination and waking at night</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy to reach with thumbs or palms; easiest lying face down</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 minutes per side</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV4 (Guanyuan), BL40 (Weizhong), <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">KI1 (Yongquan)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Recent back injury or surgery, or unexplained acute back pain — see a doctor first (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why BL23 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">BL23 is the back-shu point of the Kidney, meaning it is the point on the back where the kidney's qi is said to gather. It sits on the Bladder meridian, which runs in two parallel bands beside the spine. In traditional Chinese medicine the kidneys are regarded as the root of vitality and are classically said to govern the low back and knees, the ears, and reproductive energy. Because of this, BL23 is one of the first points practitioners reach for when a person presents with low back pain, weak knees, fatigue, or low energy.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The point is easy to locate without any equipment because it sits at a consistent landmark — the level of the second lumbar vertebra (L2), which roughly matches the height of the belly button. For someone who spends long hours at a desk or standing, self-massage at BL23 can bring quick local relief to the lower back, and the area responds well to warmth. Along with KI1 and CV4, BL23 is considered a foundational point for supporting energy and resilience in this system, and it is the traditional partner of BL40 for lumbar complaints.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Where Is This Point Located?</h2>
    <img src="/images/bl23-shenshu-location.webp" alt="BL23 acupressure point location on the lower back beside the second lumbar vertebra" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="1080" height="896" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">BL23 is on the lower back, on the Bladder meridian, 1.5 cun lateral to the lower border of the spinous process of the second lumbar vertebra (L2) — about two finger-widths out from the spine. To find it, place a hand on your side at the height of your belly button and trace that level horizontally around to your spine; the vertebra at that line is approximately L2. Feel for the small gap between the spinous processes, then move about two finger-widths out from the midline. You should feel a slightly fleshy area between the spine and the long back muscles — that is the point.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) the level matches your belly button height (L2); (2) you are in the muscle band beside the spine, not on the spinous process; (3) pressing toward the spine produces a deep, dull, achy warmth rather than a sharp pinch on bone.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use BL23 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-forest-200 bg-forest-50 dark:border-forest-900/40 dark:bg-forest-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• Your lower back feels achy, stiff, or weak after sitting or standing long hours</li>
          <li>• You feel run down, fatigued, or short on energy</li>
          <li>• Your knees feel weak, or you notice ringing in the ears or waking to urinate at night</li>
          <li>• You want a daily point for grounding and stamina</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You recently injured the lower back or had spinal surgery</li>
          <li>• Back pain came on suddenly with no clear cause — get it checked first</li>
          <li>• Pain runs down the leg, or you have numbness, weakness, or loss of bladder control</li>
          <li>• There is tenderness, a rash, or broken skin over the kidney area</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: BL23 is a comfort practice for ordinary lower-back tension and fatigue, not a response to an acute back injury or unexplained pain.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Are the Benefits?</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Low back pain:</strong> BL23 is a primary point for lower backache, stiffness, and weakness in the lumbar region.</li>
      <li><strong>Kidney and energy support:</strong> Traditionally used to support vitality, stamina, and the body's reserves of essential energy.</li>
      <li><strong>Fatigue:</strong> Used for general tiredness and low energy, especially when linked to a sense of inner depletion.</li>
      <li><strong>Tinnitus and hearing:</strong> Classically linked to the kidneys, which are said to open to the ears, so BL23 is used for ringing in the ears and declining hearing.</li>
      <li><strong>Frequent urination and nocturia:</strong> Traditional use for urinary frequency and waking at night to urinate.</li>
      <li><strong>Knee weakness:</strong> Included in routines for weak knees, legs, and the traditional "loins" region.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How Do You Use This Point?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">This point is best worked while lying face down, with the pressure directed toward the spine. It responds well to both thumbs working together and to the heel of the palm for a broader, warmer pressure.</p>
    <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Step-by-step technique</h3>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Lie face down on a firm surface with a pillow under your hips to soften the lower back.</li>
      <li>Find L2 by tracing the level of your belly button around to your spine, then move two finger-widths out on both sides.</li>
      <li>Place your thumbs (or the heels of your palms) on both BL23 points.</li>
      <li>Press gently at first, then deepen the pressure and knead toward the spine in slow circles for 1–2 minutes.</li>
      <li>Repeat daily, and finish with a warm compress or heat pack over the area for extra relief.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing directly on the spinous process of the spine itself. The target is the muscle band about two finger-widths out from the vertebra — if you feel hard bone under your thumbs, slide outward and stay on the fleshy muscle.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine BL23 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A full lower-back and energy routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Low back pain or stiffness</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL23 → BL40 (Weizhong) → BL23 again, 30–60s each, repeat 1–2 times daily</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Fatigue or low energy</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL23 → <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">KI1</a> → CV4 (Guanyuan), holding each 60s, ideally before bed</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Weak knees or legs</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL23 → <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">KI1</a> → <a href="/points/li11-quchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LI11</a>, then gentle knee bends</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Tinnitus or waking at night</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL23 → <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">KI1</a> → <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">HT7</a>, in the evening for a calming close to the day</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-back-pain/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for back pain</a> and the <a href="/body/back-acupressure-points/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">back acupressure points</a> body guide.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The point is often tender, and the lower back usually feels looser and warmer within a few minutes. Many people describe a grounding, calming effect rather than an instant energy spike. Do not expect a cure after one press.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, backache often feels less constant and energy settles into a steadier pattern. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for low back pain, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For ongoing fatigue or chronic lower-back tension, keep BL23 as one tool alongside movement, sleep, and professional care. Pain that has not improved after 2–3 weeks, pain running down a leg, numbness, or any change in bladder or bowel control is a signal to see a clinician.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Does the Research Say?</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure for pain conditions, including low back pain, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Much of the research on back-related acupressure has focused on points along the Bladder meridian, and BL23 is among the most frequently cited for lumbar support in both clinical and self-care settings. Our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/">overview of the acupressure evidence</a> gives a balanced summary of what the research does and does not show.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Overall, current evidence suggests acupressure may help with discomfort and fatigue, but study designs vary widely and many trials are limited by small samples or the difficulty of creating a convincing sham treatment. We therefore describe the effects as supportive rather than guaranteed. Persistent low back pain, urinary symptoms, or numbness in the legs should always be evaluated by a clinician.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["chen-wang-2014"]} note="The review above evaluated acupressure for pain, which is the context where BL23 is most often used. It is not a study of BL23 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Are There Safety Concerns?</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Press the muscle band beside the vertebra, not the spine itself — never apply direct pressure to bone.</li>
      <li>Avoid strong pressure if you have an acute back injury, fracture, or recent spinal surgery.</li>
      <li>Do not self-treat unexplained, sudden, or severe back pain — get a diagnosis first.</li>
      <li>Skip the point if there is skin irritation, a rash, or tenderness over the kidney area.</li>
      <li>Pregnant women should use gentle, brief pressure on the lower back and consult a care provider first.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Back pain that came on suddenly with no clear cause, or after a fall or heavy lift</li>
        <li>Pain, numbness, or tingling running down a leg, or weakness in a foot or leg</li>
        <li>Loss of bladder or bowel control, or numbness in the groin area — seek urgent care</li>
        <li>Pain with fever, unexplained weight loss, or a recent illness</li>
        <li>Lower back pain that has not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular practice</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find BL23?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Trace the height of your belly button horizontally around to your spine — the vertebra at that line is roughly L2. BL23 sits about two finger-widths out from the spine at that level, in the muscle band beside the vertebra. You should feel it as a slightly tender or fleshy spot.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can BL23 help with low back pain?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">BL23 is a primary point for lower backache and stiffness in this tradition, and it is often combined with BL40 for lumbar complaints. It may help as a supportive self-care measure, but persistent or radiating back pain should be assessed by a clinician.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press BL23?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Knead both sides toward the spine for 1–2 minutes at a time, once or twice daily. The area responds well to warmth, so finish with a heat pack or warm compress. Consistency (daily) matters more than a single long session.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is BL23 safe to press?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes, for most people, as long as you work the muscle beside the spine rather than pressing on bone and you start gently. Avoid it over skin irritation, after recent back surgery, or if you have an acute injury. Pregnant women should use light pressure and check with a care provider.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of pressing BL23?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for sudden back pain with no clear cause, pain or numbness running down a leg, weakness, or any loss of bladder or bowel control. Unexplained urinary changes, ringing in the ears, or back pain that persists beyond 2–3 weeks also deserve a medical opinion.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to All Points</a>
    </div>
